#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    int N, M;
    if (scanf("%d %d", &N, &M) != 2) return 0;

    /* 
       We use 2D imos on rows:
       For each row r, covered columns form an interval [L, R].
       For a triangle (a,b,x), rows a..a+x are covered, and on row r=a+t:
         columns b..b+t
       This can be represented by adding on transformed coordinates:
         u = row
         left boundary  = b
         right boundary = b + (u-a)
       We maintain for each row difference arrays for starts/ends of intervals.

       Simpler:
       For each triangle and each affected row, add interval [b, b+t].
       Since constraints are not given here, use O(total area) only if safe?
       Better use 2D difference on triangular grid.

       Let D1[a][b] affect vertical accumulation,
           D2[a][b] affect diagonal accumulation,
           then coverage C can be reconstructed by:
             C[a][b] = C[a-1][b] + C[a-1][b-1] - C[a-2][b-1] + D[a][b]
       However an easier standard method for such triangular range updates:
       For triangle with top (a,b), size x:
         diff[a][b]++
         diff[a+x+1][b]--
         diff[a+x+1][b+x+1]++
         diff[a][b+x+1]--
       and reconstruct by triangular prefix:
         s[a][b] = diff[a][b] + s[a-1][b] + s[a][b-1] - s[a-1][b-1]
       This works on oblique coordinates if we map cell (a,b) in a rectangular array.
       Here points covered are exactly nails inside/on triangle:
         {(r,c): a<=r<=a+x, b<=c<=b+(r-a)}
       Using coordinates p=r, q=c, condition q-p <= b-a.
       Transform:
         X = r
         Y = c-r+N   (shift)
       Then triangle becomes rectangle:
         X in [a, a+x], Y in [b-a+N, b-a+N]
       Not enough; need also c<=b+x => no.

       So instead use row-interval events, O(sum x). This is acceptable for moderate N,
       but to be safer, aggregate with row difference arrays.
       For each row r, interval [b, b+r-a].
       We can process triangle by two row-events:
         starting at row a: interval starts with L=b, R=b
         each next row increases R by 1 until row a+x.
       Use two difference tables over rows/cols:
         add constant to L-prefix and diagonal-like to R-prefix.

       Easier robust method:
       For each row, maintain diff over columns.
       For triangle (a,b,x):
         for r=a..a+x:
            rowdiff[r][b]++
            rowdiff[r][b+(r-a)+1]--
       Complexity O(sum(x+1)).
    */

    int **diff = (int **)malloc((N + 2) * sizeof(int *));
    for (int i = 0; i <= N + 1; i++) {
        diff[i] = (int *)calloc(N + 3, sizeof(int));
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < M; i++) {
        int a, b, x;
        scanf("%d %d %d", &a, &b, &x);
        for (int r = a; r <= a + x; r++) {
            int L = b;
            int R = b + (r - a);
            diff[r][L] += 1;
            diff[r][R + 1] -= 1;
        }
    }

    long long ans = 0;
    for (int r = 1; r <= N; r++) {
        int cur = 0;
        for (int c = 1; c <= r; c++) {
            cur += diff[r][c];
            if (cur > 0) ans++;
        }
    }

    printf("%lld\n", ans);

    for (int i = 0; i <= N + 1; i++) free(diff[i]);
    free(diff);

    return 0;
}

